##Large Language Models
A language model is a probabilistic model that predicts the probability of the next token based on the previous context.
The large in the large language model refers to the number of parameters.

For example:

“They sent me a ___”

The model produces a probability distribution over its vocabulary:

dog → 0.45
cat → 0.20
lion → 0.03
elephant → 0.02
etc.

The model can assign probabilities to the tokens in its vocabulary. It then selects a token according to the generation strategy. In the simplified example from the lesson, the token with the highest probability is selected.

The selected token is added to the input, and the model predicts the next token again:

Predict → Append token → Predict again → Repeat

When the model generates EOS (End of Sequence), generation stops.
